- Passage: Knuth TAOCP §2.4 + §2.2.6 — Multilinked structures / orthogonal lists (Top-20 entry #2)
- Repo on box: ~/toapdd/sparse (fresh clone of wbic16/libphext-rs, v0.4.0; no existing tests modified; nothing pushed)
- Test file: tests/toapdd_sparse.rs on box; local source ~/workspace/toapdd/tests/sparse.rs (SHA-256 ee8580dfb334adb24f8c1454a9e2359aabdb2baca6b36d64bf15f5a9f727b4c3, identical on box)
- Thermal: rock-mountain 48.9 °C at dispatch check, 37.8 °C after the run — no thermal hold.

## Concept

A sparse matrix stored as a phext: one non-zero cell = one scroll, at a
coordinate that carries both its row and its column. Absent cells have
no scroll at all.

Encoding (documented in the test): cell (row, col) → coordinate
`1.1.1/1.1.1/<row+1>.<col+1>.1` (chapter = row+1, section = col+1,
scroll = 1; components are ≥ 1, so 0-based indices shift by +1).
Fixture: 4×5 matrix, 6 non-zero cells out of 20.

## Exact cargo counts

```
cargo test --test toapdd_sparse
running 6 tests
test absent_cells_occupy_no_scroll ... ok
test column_traversal_recovers_the_matrix ... ok
test fetch_by_coordinate_is_cell_lookup ... ok
test inserting_one_cell_touches_one_coordinate ... ok
test row_traversal_recovers_the_matrix ... ok
test row_and_column_traversals_reconstruct_the_same_dense_matrix ... ok
test result: ok. 6 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out
```

## What the tests prove

- **Absent cells occupy no scroll:** `explode`/`phokenize` (empties filtered) yield exactly 6 populated scrolls for a 20-cell matrix; every populated coordinate decodes back to a declared non-zero cell.
- **Fetch by coordinate = cell lookup:** all 20 (row, col) lookups via `fetch` match the declared matrix — value for non-zero cells, `""` for absent cells.
- **Inserting one cell touches one coordinate:** inserting (1,2) grows the populated set by exactly 1, adds only that coordinate, removes none, and leaves every pre-existing cell byte-identical (explode-map comparison before/after).
- **Row traversal recovers the matrix:** grouping populated scrolls by chapter (row) and ordering by section (column) reproduces each row exactly, and agrees cell-by-cell with `fetch`.
- **Column traversal recovers the matrix:** grouping by section (column) and ordering by chapter (row) — the orthogonal direction — reproduces each column exactly, and agrees with `fetch`.
- **Both traversals agree:** dense 4×5 grids reconstructed from the row grouping and from the column grouping are identical and equal the declared matrix, with `""` precisely at absent cells.

## Enhancement over Knuth

One non-zero = one scroll; its coordinate carries row+column at once; no dense array, no special sparse encoding — orthogonal links become coordinate references (same chapter = row link, same section = column link) that can be read, diffed, and hosted as text, with no pointer fields stored anywhere.

## API gaps

None. The public libphext 0.4.0 API (`fetch`, `insert`, `explode`, `implode`, `phokenize`, `to_coordinate`) was sufficient for every claim in the spec, as written. Notes, not gaps: empties must be filtered when counting populated cells (`phokenize("")` surfaces an empty scroll, per the cheat sheet), and matrix indices need the +1 shift because coordinate components are ≥ 1.
